<speak>
<p>Continue in the same evaluation ring A, with u odd and greater than one. At each subset root zeta sub S, abbreviate the evaluated polynomials by c sub S, d sub S, and g sub S. The two ratio families are r sub S equal to d sub S over c sub S, and w sub S equal to g sub S over c sub S.<break time="600ms"/>The sign array already supplied exact polynomial quotients: d minus c divided by two, and g minus c divided by one plus i. First perform that coefficientwise division. Then evaluate at zeta sub S. Finally multiply by the unit inverse of c sub S. Both resulting elements belong to A. We have introduced no inverse of two or of one plus i.<break time="1000ms"/></p>
<p>Write each ratio as one plus its difference from one. For the r family, the multiplier is two; for the w family, it is one plus i. Call the corresponding divided difference x sub S. Both rows now have the exact form one plus t times x sub S, with x sub S in A. The value of t and the elements x sub S depend on the family.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>Start with two factors. Choose one or t x one from the first factor, and one or t x two from the second. The four choices give one, t x one, t x two, and t squared x one x two. Collect the two linear terms. This is an equality: one plus t times the sum of x one and x two, plus t squared x one x two.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>With three factors, choose either one or t x from each factor again. Choosing no x gives one. Choosing exactly one gives t times the sum of the three x values. Choosing two gives three quadratic terms. Choosing all three gives the cubic term. Keep those last four terms together. They equal t squared times Q, where Q is the sum of the three pairwise products, plus t times the triple product. Nothing has been discarded.<break time="1000ms"/></p>
<p>The same grouping works for any finite collection. The product equals one, plus t times the sum of all x values, plus t squared Q, with Q in A. Every remaining term contains at least two copies of t. This is an exact remainder, not an approximation. For one factor, Q is zero.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>Apply this identity to each side of the even-subset and odd-subset product equality separately. Keep their linear sums and their remainders distinct. Use t equal to two for the r ratios and t equal to one plus i for the w ratios, with the corresponding x values in each case.<break time="600ms"/>For u equal to fifteen, each side has two factors. For u equal to three, each side has just one, so both remainders vanish. The next step subtracts the two expanded products and cancels a common factor in the original ring before any modular reduction.<break time="1200ms"/></p>
</speak>
